- translation and rotation;
- infinite-line intersection.

Geometry is unit-agnostic. Every operation requires coordinates expressed in one
consistent linear unit. Unit conversion belongs to the PEK Units Engine.

# PEK-09 Hydraulics & Drainage Engine — Wave 1 v1.0

This domain extension introduces 30 generic SI-based functions for:

- rainfall depth, rainfall volume and runoff volume;
- Rational Method peak flow;
- weighted runoff coefficients;
- circular and rectangular flow geometry;
- hydraulic radius;
- Manning and Chezy velocity/discharge;
- full-pipe discharge;
- flow velocity and travel time;
- detention storage and detention time;
- infiltration volume and required infiltration area;
- Darcy flow;
- pump sizing and outlet counts;
- water balance and continuity error;
- pipe fill ratio;
- drainage-specific discharge;
- roof outlet flow;
- required pipe area and equivalent diameter.

Project-specific climate datasets, local authority rules and design standards
remain external configuration inputs.

# PEK Loads Wave 1 v1.0

Implements the first 30 Loads functions in the Engineering Kernel registry.

Capabilities include:

- generic load records and normalized directions;
- dead, imposed, wind, snow, seismic and thermal loads;
- hydrostatic and earth-pressure calculations;
- line-load, area-load and point-load transformations;
- uniform, triangular and trapezoidal resultants;
- moments and vector components;
- load scaling and vector summation;
- resultant loads;
- scalar and vector load combinations;
- characteristic, design and accidental combinations;
- dynamic amplification.

No regulatory factors are hard-coded. Partial factors, combination factors and
national annex values must be supplied by future standards layers.

# PEK Materials Wave 1 v1.0

Implements the first 30 Materials functions in the Engineering Kernel registry.

Capabilities include:

- generic and domain-specific material records;
- concrete, structural steel, reinforcement steel, timber and masonry;
- aluminium, glass, plastics and soil;
- shear modulus, bulk modulus and specific weight;
- stress, strain and linear elastic transformations;
- characteristic and design values;
- safety factors and utilization;
- temperature and moisture factors;
- thermal strain and thermal expansion;
- creep-adjusted modulus and shrinkage deformation;
- classification, validation and immutable material adjustment.

The module does not hard-code regulatory design classes. Code-specific material
tables and national annex values belong in future standards/data layers.

# PEK Mathematics Wave 1 v1.0

This release implements PEK-MATH-0001 through PEK-MATH-0025.

Implemented capabilities:

- deterministic arithmetic;
- powers and roots;
- minimum, maximum and clamping;
- arithmetic and weighted means;
- percentages and percentage change;
- factors, margins and utilization;
- tolerance-based comparison;
- decimal and increment rounding;
- normalization and linear interpolation;
- dot product, vector magnitude and vector normalization.

Invalid mathematical domains, zero denominators, incompatible vector lengths,
empty inputs and non-finite values are rejected explicitly.

# PEK Numerical Test Policy v1.1b

Version 1.1b repairs all remaining incompatible `assert_float_close` call sites
using an AST-based repository scan rather than hard-coded file and line edits.

Repairs are selected from the expected value:

- numeric tuple/list -> `assert_numeric_sequence_close`;
- string, bytes, boolean, integer or `None` -> `assertEqual`;
- scalar float -> remains `assert_float_close`;
- dynamic or unknown value -> remains unchanged.

A dedicated call-site checker now prevents future use of scalar float assertions
for explicit sequences or exact literals.

# PEK-11 Reinforced Concrete Design Engine — Wave 1 v1.0

This domain extension introduces 30 generic SI-based reinforced-concrete
design functions.

Coverage:

- concrete design strength, tensile strength and elastic modulus;
- concrete unit weight and section self-weight;
- reinforcement design strength, bar area and reinforcement ratio;
- effective depth, lever arm and required tension reinforcement;
- minimum and maximum reinforcement;
- flexural utilization;
- concrete shear stress and shear capacity;
- required shear reinforcement and stirrup spacing;
- shear utilization;
- concrete, steel and combined axial column capacity;
- column slenderness and axial-moment interaction index;
- crack-control ratio and deflection utilization;
- nominal cover, anchorage length and development length;
- serviceability pass/fail evaluation.

The module intentionally does not embed a national design code. Partial factors,
detailing limits, durability classes and national annexes remain configurable
through Phoenix project and policy layers.

# PEK-08 Structural Engine — Wave 1 v1.0

The first Structural Engine wave adds 30 generic structural-mechanics
functions in SI units.

Implemented areas:

- rectangular and circular section properties;
- parallel-axis theorem;
- axial, bending, combined and average shear stress;
- strain and axial deformation;
- simply supported beam reactions, moments and deflections;
- cantilever deflection and rotation;
- effective length, slenderness and Euler buckling;
- second-order moment amplification;
- utilization and factor of safety;
- 2D force resultants;
- local/global 2D vector transformations.

The module deliberately avoids hard-coded national-code factors. Those belong
in later steel, concrete, timber and masonry code layers.

# PEK Validation Wave 1 v1.0

Implements the first 30 Validation functions in the Engineering Kernel registry.

Capabilities include:

- type, required-field, NaN and finite-value validation;
- range, positivity, choice, length and dimension checks;
- unit, consistency and monotonicity checks;
- absolute and relative numerical tolerances;
- rounding and convergence validation;
- factor-of-safety and utilization validation;
- material, geometry and load plausibility checks;
- dependency, traceability and registry validation;
- issue severity classification;
- structured validation issues and reports;
- report merging and machine-readable summaries.

Code-specific engineering rules remain external and can be layered on top of
these generic validation primitives.

# Knowledge Graph Usage

Phoenix uses the Knowledge Graph to connect:

- project objects;
- requirements;
- standards;
- assumptions;
- calculations;
- documents;
- drawings;
- permits;
- decisions;
- source evidence.

Typical relations include `contains`, `documents`, `derived_from`,
`validated_by`, `requires`, `conflicts_with` and `supersedes`.

# QGIS Integration Usage

BB12 supports two runtime modes.

## Offline mode

Available everywhere. It provides:

- GIS project manifests;
- layer registration;
- GeoJSON handling;
- CRS and extent validation;
- basic spatial primitives;
- QGIS `.qgs` generation;
- Digital Twin and Knowledge Graph publication.

## Native mode

Activated when PyQGIS or `qgis_process` is available. Future Build Blocks can
use this mode for Processing algorithms, raster analysis and native QGIS export.

Project workflows must record the runtime mode in their evidence.

# AI Workflow Engine Usage

A Phoenix AI workflow consists of:

- a project context;
- ordered capabilities;
- explicit dependencies;
- optional conditions;
- bounded retries;
- assumptions;
- evidence;
- decision records.

Workflow steps should remain small, deterministic where practical, and
individually testable. External engineering engines are invoked through adapters,
not embedded directly into workflow definitions.

